                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "4575813e-9a24-42e1-ab61-993e8186b98a",
                        "source_entry_id": "69fadd49-d82e-3d0e-aef8-7c6a13fa330d",
                        "source_sense_id": "58a4b5b9-6409-323d-8140-2473ff3b6fe9",
                        "target_lang_word": "caiman",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "caiman",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "e3660e3d-4e7b-3aac-a3ca-9c2eac1af16e",
                                        "entry_id": "d3540d3b-56ba-3e5c-9ab4-20ea29eb56ac",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Any of the relatively small crocodilians of the genus Caiman, within the family Alligatoridae."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "5fde0122-b65b-4968-8268-d09c83896671",
                                                "sense_id": "e3660e3d-4e7b-3aac-a3ca-9c2eac1af16e",
                                                "text": "Caimans are reptiles that are closely related to their Central and South American neighbors, the alligators. Adult caimans are usually four to six feet (1.8 m) in length."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "93e280fc-3d42-44e2-9473-120522a5636b",
                                                "sense_id": "e3660e3d-4e7b-3aac-a3ca-9c2eac1af16e",
                                                "text": "2002, International Wildlife Encyclopedia: Brown bear - Cheetah, 3rd Edition, page 358,\nThe caimans are found in South America, mainly in the Amazon basin, with one species extending into the southern part of Mexico and another reaching northern parts of Argentina."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "0cc31eac-cab0-47d4-bcf4-7c59efe32c85",
                                                "sense_id": "e3660e3d-4e7b-3aac-a3ca-9c2eac1af16e",
                                                "text": "Newly hatched caimans eat insects. Young caimans are eaten by Jabirus, Wood Storks, Great Egrets, and raccoons. Adult caimans have no predators except human poachers."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "f560281a-1e15-367b-bea5-5c05d4cd4319",
                                        "entry_id": "d3540d3b-56ba-3e5c-9ab4-20ea29eb56ac",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A crocodilian of the subfamily Caimaninae, which includes the very large black caiman."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "broadly"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "5f1c2ce0-ada2-3502-9c15-47247352149d",
                                        "entry_id": "d3540d3b-56ba-3e5c-9ab4-20ea29eb56ac",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "A semi-aquatic lizard, of the genus Dracaena, found in South America. To differentiate from caimans, they are referred to as caiman lizards."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "e7f6cb99-1005-4bed-9c85-6425b37b76cf",
                        "source_entry_id": "69fadd49-d82e-3d0e-aef8-7c6a13fa330d",
                        "source_sense_id": "58a4b5b9-6409-323d-8140-2473ff3b6fe9",
                        "target_lang_word": "cayman",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "cayman",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "fc0d5d28-82d7-3a40-897a-8a270578e07b",
                                        "entry_id": "0418e94d-88ee-3a8b-811d-281c10cb1522",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Alternative spelling of caiman."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [
                                            "alt-of",
                                            "alternative"
                                        ],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "510010ec-bac1-460c-855b-43396d9b4ced",
                                                "sense_id": "fc0d5d28-82d7-3a40-897a-8a270578e07b",
                                                "text": "We were about to follow this advice, when another man, more rash than his comrades, said, 'I'm not afraid of caymans,' and spurred his horse into the stream."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "f11948f7-c321-4c38-afbc-a1b86c715577",
                                                "sense_id": "fc0d5d28-82d7-3a40-897a-8a270578e07b",
                                                "text": "With a dull, heavy splash an occasional cayman plunged in from the bank."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "67eaecb6-39c2-47a6-a7a7-12718f84d229",
                                                "sense_id": "fc0d5d28-82d7-3a40-897a-8a270578e07b",
                                                "text": "Surely, when this animal comes, it is to eat us. Thus, we have to sing this hoa. This is how I learned this hoa for caymans. One sings this so he won't be eaten by the caymans."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "ebcd5c86-758c-4401-bede-8148ed8f9045",
                                                "sense_id": "fc0d5d28-82d7-3a40-897a-8a270578e07b",
                                                "text": "Laguna Tortuguero has some 2,000 caimanes (caymans), a type of alligator indigenous to Central and S America."
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "translation_id": "1c4506b5-fc44-48b6-9f86-a08904de83e2",
                        "source_entry_id": "69fadd49-d82e-3d0e-aef8-7c6a13fa330d",
